<HR>
<H1>15  The planner</H1>
<HR>
The Planner was the page of Lotus Organizer people remember: a whole year on one spread, a row for each month
and a column for each day, with coloured bars across it for holidays, training courses and projects. You
chose a colour from the key, dragged across the days, and the bar was painted. This chapter builds it, and
puts its bars in the Diary too. It's mostly drawing - a grid, bars, patterns - with one new kind of drag and
one new drawing technique, clipping. The result is <CODE>!Org15</CODE>.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O15Planner" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Planning the planner</H2>
<DL>
<DT>What it remembers
<DD>Bars. A bar is a kind (one of six, each with its own colour) and the first and last days it covers.<P>
<DT>What can happen
<DD>A bar is painted by choosing a kind in the key and dragging across days, or added with the New button;
it is deleted with the wastebasket or the Delete key; the year is turned with the page corners. Bars show in
the Diary on the days they cover, and double-clicking a day on the chart turns the Diary to it.<P>
</DL>
So a bar is as small as an entry can be:<P>
<PRE>
{ id: 4, key: 3, from: '2026-10-05', to: '2026-10-23' }
</PRE>
<CODE>key</CODE> is the kind, a number from 0 to 5; the kinds' names are kept in one place, in
<CODE>Store</CODE>, with the collection:<P>
<PRE>
// A planner bar: { key (0-5), from, to } - the days it covers
export const PLANNER_KEY = ['Holiday', 'Training', 'Meeting',
  'Project A', 'Project B', 'Away'];
export class Planner extends Collection {
  onDay(date) {
    return this.all().filter((b) =&gt; b.from &lt;= date &amp;&amp; date &lt;= b.to);
  }
}
</PRE>
<CODE>onDay</CODE> gives the bars covering a day. Since day keys sort as strings in date order (chapter 11),
"the day is between <CODE>from</CODE> and <CODE>to</CODE>" is two string comparisons. Check tests a day inside a
bar and the day after it:<P>
<PRE>
  const planner = new Planner([{ id: 1, key: 0, from: '2026-08-01',
    to: '2026-08-14' }]);
  check('planner: a bar covers its days',
    [planner.onDay('2026-08-07').length, planner.onDay('2026-08-15')
      .length], [1, 0]);
</PRE>
and the Document makes the collection from the file (until now it was a plain <CODE>Collection</CODE>, kept and saved but never shown):<P>
<PRE>
    this.planner = new Planner(data.planner);
</PRE>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>A chart of the year</H2>
January to June go on the left-hand page and July to December on the right, a row for each month and 31
columns for the days, with the month's short name down the left. The size of a row and a column comes from the
size of the page, so the same code draws both halves; <CODE>grid</CODE> works it out:<P>
<PRE>
const TOP = 58, LABEL = 34, KEYH = 44;

export class PlannerPage extends Section {
  constructor(org) {
    super(org, { id: 'planner', name: 'Planner', colour: '#b4a7d6',
      newHelp: 'add a planner bar' });
    this.year = +D.today().slice(0, 4);
    this.pen = 0;                         // the colour to paint with
  }
  state() { return { year: this.year }; }

  /** The chart's layout on page p: row height, cell width, origin. */
  grid(p, first) {
    const rowH = Math.floor((p.h - TOP - KEYH - 10) / 6);
    const cellW = (p.w - LABEL - 16) / 31;
    return { rowH, cellW, x0: p.x + LABEL + 8, y0: p.y + TOP, first };
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>TOP</CODE> leaves room for the heading and the day numbers, <CODE>LABEL</CODE> for the month names and
<CODE>KEYH</CODE> for the key at the bottom. What's left is shared between six rows and 31 columns.
<CODE>first</CODE> is the first month on the page: 1 on the left, 7 on the right.<P>
<CODE>draw</CODE> draws the two halves with the same function:<P>
<PRE>
  draw(g, L, R) {
    heading(g, L, 'Planner', this.colour, String(this.year));
    heading(g, R, String(this.year), this.colour);
    this.keys = [];
    this.half(g, L, 1);
    this.half(g, R, 7);
  }
  half(g, p, first) {
    const G = this.grid(p, first);
    g.font = '9px Homerton';
    for (let d = 1; d &lt;= 31; d += 1) {
      if (d % 5 &amp;&amp; d !== 1) continue;
      text(g, String(d), G.x0 + (d - 1) * G.cellW, G.y0 - 8, 16,
        '9px Homerton', FAINT);
    }
    for (let r = 0; r &lt; 6; r++) {
      const m = first + r, y = G.y0 + r * G.rowH;
      const day1 = `${this.year}-${String(m).padStart(2, '0')}-01`;
      text(g, D.format(day1, '%M3'), p.x + 8, y + G.rowH / 2, LABEL,
        'bold 11px Homerton');
      const days = D.daysInMonth(this.year, m);
      for (let d = 1; d &lt;= days; d++) {
        const key = `${this.year}-${String(m).padStart(2, '0')}-` +
          String(d).padStart(2, '0');
        const x = G.x0 + (d - 1) * G.cellW;
        g.fillStyle = D.weekday(key) &gt;= 5 ? '#ece6d2' : '#ffffff';
        g.fillRect(x, y, G.cellW - 1, G.rowH - 2);
        if (key === D.today()) {
          g.strokeStyle = INK;
          g.strokeRect(x + 0.5, y + 0.5, G.cellW - 2, G.rowH - 3);
        }
      }
      this.bars(g, G, m, y);
    }
    // the key: three colours on each page
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
      const k = (first === 1 ? 0 : 3) + i;
      const x = p.x + 12 + i * ((p.w - 24) / 3), y = p.y + p.h - KEYH;
      barStyle(g, k, x, y + 6, 22, 16);
      if (k === this.pen) {
        g.strokeStyle = INK; g.lineWidth = 2;
        g.strokeRect(x - 3, y + 3, (p.w - 24) / 3 - 6, 22);
      }
      text(g, PLANNER_KEY[k], x + 28, y + 14, 70, '12px Homerton');
      this.keys.push({ k, x: x - 3, y: y + 3, w: (p.w - 24) / 3 - 6,
        h: 22 });
    }
  }
</PRE>
Each day is a white cell - or a pale shaded one on Saturdays and Sundays, from <CODE>D.weekday</CODE>, which
counts from 0 for Monday so the weekend is 5 and 6. Today has a box round it. Months shorter than 31 days stop
early (<CODE>D.daysInMonth</CODE>), leaving a gap at the end of their row. The day keys are built from the year,
the month and the day with <CODE>padStart</CODE> - <CODE>'2026-09-05'</CODE>, not <CODE>'2026-9-5'</CODE>,
or they wouldn't sort as dates.<P>
The key at the bottom has three kinds on each page, each a small sample of its bar and its name. The one
you'll paint with (<CODE>this.pen</CODE>) has a box round it. As with entries, the page records where each
sample is, in <CODE>this.keys</CODE>, for clicks.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Drawing the bars</H2>
Each month's row is divided into six <I>lanes</I>, one for each kind, so bars of different kinds that overlap
can both be seen:<P>
<PRE>
  /** The bars in one month's row: a lane each for the six colours. */
  bars(g, G, m, y) {
    const lane = (G.rowH - 6) / 6;
    const first = `${this.year}-${String(m).padStart(2, '0')}-01`;
    const last = `${first.slice(0, 8)}${D.daysInMonth(this.year, m)}`;
    for (const b of this.doc.planner.all()) {
      if (b.to &lt; first || b.from &gt; last) continue;
      const a = b.from &lt; first ? first : b.from;
      const z = b.to &gt; last ? last : b.to;
      const x = G.x0 + (+a.slice(8) - 1) * G.cellW;
      const w = (+z.slice(8) - +a.slice(8) + 1) * G.cellW - 1;
      const by = y + 3 + b.key * lane;
      barStyle(g, b.key, x, by, w, lane - 1);
      this.box(x, by, w, lane - 1, { id: b.id });
    }
  }
</PRE>
A bar can run on from one month into the next (a holiday from 28 July to 7 August), so for each month's row
the bar is cut down to the part inside that month: from whichever is later of its first day and the 1st, to
whichever is earlier of its last day and the month's last day - comparing day keys again. Bars that don't touch
the month at all are skipped with <CODE>continue</CODE>. Each bar drawn is recorded with <CODE>this.box</CODE>,
so it can be chosen, dragged to the wastebasket or linked, like any other entry. (A bar across two months is
recorded twice, once for each part; both have the same ref.)<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Colour and pattern</H2>
The Style Guide's rule from chapter 13 matters most here: six colours side by side are exactly what people
with colour-blindness can't tell apart, and a grey printout loses them altogether. So every kind has a
pattern as well as a colour - Holiday plain, Training diagonal stripes, Meeting dots, Project A upright
stripes, Project B stripes the other way, Away a line along the middle. One function fills a rectangle in a
kind's colour and pattern, and the key, the chart and the Diary all use it:<P>
<PRE>
export const KEY_COLOURS = ['#e06666', '#6fa8dc', '#93c47d',
  '#f6b26b', '#b4a7d6', '#999999'];

/** Fill a rectangle in a planner colour, with its pattern. */
export function barStyle(g, k, x, y, w, h) {
  g.fillStyle = KEY_COLOURS[k];
  g.fillRect(x, y, w, h);
  g.save();
  g.beginPath(); g.rect(x, y, w, h); g.clip();
  g.strokeStyle = 'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)';
  g.fillStyle = 'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)';
  g.lineWidth = 1;
  for (let i = -h; i &lt; w + h; i += 5) {
    g.beginPath();
    if (k === 1) { g.moveTo(x + i, y + h); g.lineTo(x + i + h, y); }
    if (k === 2) g.fillRect(x + i, y + h / 2 - 1, 2, 2);
    if (k === 3) { g.moveTo(x + i, y); g.lineTo(x + i, y + h); }
    if (k === 4) { g.moveTo(x + i, y); g.lineTo(x + i + h, y + h); }
    if (k === 5) { g.moveTo(x, y + h / 2 + 0.5); g.lineTo(x + w,
      y + h / 2 + 0.5); }
    g.stroke();
  }
  g.restore();
}
</PRE>
The pattern's lines are drawn right across the rectangle and beyond, and then cut off at its edges. That's
<I>clipping</I>, which chapter 2's ListView used to keep each column's text inside its column. Here it is again,
with the two calls that go round it:<P>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>g.save()</CODE> remembers everything about how the canvas is drawing - colours, line width, and the
clipping area.<P>
<LI><CODE>g.beginPath(); g.rect(x, y, w, h); g.clip();</CODE> makes the rectangle the clipping area: nothing
drawn from now on appears outside it.<P>
<LI><CODE>g.restore()</CODE> puts everything back as it was at <CODE>save</CODE>, so the rest of the page isn't
clipped.<P>
</UL>
Without clipping, the stripes would have to be worked out to stop exactly at the edges, which is much harder.
The patterns are drawn in black at 45% strength, <CODE>'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)'</CODE> - a colour you can see
through, as in chapter 11's answer 2 - so they darken whatever colour is underneath.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Painting a bar</H2>
A click on a sample in the key chooses the kind to paint with:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the mouse
  click(ev) {
    const k = this.keys?.find((r) =&gt; inside(r, ev.x, ev.y));
    if (k) { this.pen = k.k; this.org.redraw(); }
    return true;
  }
  /** Drag across days to paint a bar in the chosen colour. */
  async drag(ev) {
    const from = this.dayAt(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (!from) return false;
    const drop = await wimp.drag({ event: ev.pointerEvent,
      type: 'point' });
    const to = drop.window === this.org.win
      ? this.dayAt(drop.x, drop.y) : null;
    if (!to) return true;
    const [a, z] = from &lt;= to ? [from, to] : [to, from];
    this.doc.planner.add({ key: this.pen, from: a, to: z });
    return true;
  }
</PRE>
Dragging across days is a different kind of drag from chapter 12's. There, you dragged an <I>entry</I>, and
<CODE>Tools.drag</CODE> handled it: if the drag starts on an entry's box, it moves it or bins it. Here the drag
starts on an empty day, so <CODE>Tools.drag</CODE> finds no entry and hands the drag to the section's own
<CODE>drag</CODE>. (So a drag that starts on a bar drags the bar - to the wastebasket, say - and one that
starts on an empty day paints.) The Planner's asks the Wimp for a
<CODE>'point'</CODE> drag - the pointer on its own, with nothing carried - and waits for where it ends:<P>
<PRE>
const drop = await wimp.drag({ event: ev.pointerEvent,
  type: 'point' });
</PRE>
If it ends on the organiser's window, <CODE>dayAt</CODE> gives the day there. The drag might have gone left
instead of right, so the two days are put in order with array destructuring (chapter 3), taking the two
days in the right order from one of two arrays:<P>
<PRE>
const [a, z] = from &lt;= to ? [from, to] : [to, from];
</PRE>
and a bar of the chosen kind is added. As always, the page doesn't draw it: the collection changes, and the
window redraws.<P>
<H3>Which day?</H3>
<CODE>dayAt</CODE> turns a point back into a day, trying each half in turn:<P>
<PRE>
  dayAt(x, y) {
    for (const [p, first] of [[this.org.pages[0], 1],
      [this.org.pages[1], 7]]) {
      const G = this.grid(p, first);
      const row = Math.floor((y - G.y0) / G.rowH);
      const col = Math.floor((x - G.x0) / G.cellW);
      if (row &lt; 0 || row &gt; 5 || col &lt; 0 || col &gt; 30) continue;
      if (x &lt; p.x || x &gt; p.x + p.w) continue;
      const m = first + row;
      if (col + 1 &gt; D.daysInMonth(this.year, m)) return null;
      const two = (n) =&gt; String(n).padStart(2, '0');
      return `${this.year}-${two(m)}-${two(col + 1)}`;
    }
    return null;
  }
</PRE>
It's the grid sums the other way round: dividing by the row height and column width gives the row and column,
and the row and the half's first month give the month. A point beyond the end of a short month (the 31st of
June) is no day at all.<P>
<H3>Without the mouse</H3>
Dragging isn't for everyone, so New (the button, or Insert) asks for a bar in a form: the kind from a pop-up
menu, and the first and last days:<P>
<PRE>
  async newEntry() {
    this.form ??= new Form(this.org.app.task, { title: 'Planner bar',
      buttons: ['Cancel', 'OK'], fields: [
        { name: 'key', label: 'Kind', width: 140,
          choices: PLANNER_KEY },
        { name: 'from', label: 'From', width: 120 },
        { name: 'to', label: 'To', width: 120 }],
      check: (v) =&gt; (!D.parseDay(v.from) ? { field: 'from',
        message: 'Give the date as 25/09/2026' }
        : !D.parseDay(v.to) ? { field: 'to',
          message: 'Give the date as 25/09/2026' } : null) });
    const v = await this.form.ask({ key: PLANNER_KEY[this.pen] });
    this.org.focus();
    if (!v) return;
    const [a, z] = [D.parseDay(v.from), D.parseDay(v.to)].sort();
    this.doc.planner.add({ key: PLANNER_KEY.indexOf(v.key), from: a,
      to: z });
  }
</PRE>
The form is made once and kept (<CODE>??=</CODE> makes it the first time only). The two dates are sorted
with <CODE>sort()</CODE> - day keys again - so it doesn't matter which way round they're typed.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Bars in the Diary</H2>
The planner feeds the Diary, as the To do section and the anniversaries do. The Diary's
<CODE>itemsOn</CODE> gets a fourth source, the bars covering the day, first in the list:<P>
<PRE>
  /** Everything to show on a day: bars, anniversaries, to-dos, then
   * appointments, as { kind, text, ref, time?, key? }. */
  itemsOn(key) {
    const doc = this.doc, year = +key.slice(0, 4);
    return [
      ...doc.planner.onDay(key).map((b) =&gt; ({ kind: 'bar',
        key: b.key, text: PLANNER_KEY[b.key],
        ref: { section: 'planner', id: b.id } })),
      ...doc.anniversaries.onDay(key).map((a) =&gt; ({
        kind: 'anniversary',
        text: a.text + (nth(a, year) ? ` (${nth(a, year)})` : ''),
        ref: { section: 'anniversary', id: a.id } })),
      ...doc.todo.dueOn(key).map((t) =&gt; ({ kind: 'todo', text: t.text,
        ref: { section: 'todo', id: t.id } })),
      ...doc.diary.occurrences(key, key).map(({ appt }) =&gt; ({
        kind: 'appt', time: appt.time, text: appt.text,
        ref: { id: appt.id, date: key } })),
    ];
  }
</PRE>
The week and day views draw a bar as a little sample of its colour and pattern and its kind's name, in faint
italic; the month view, where there's less room, as a thin stripe:<P>
<PRE>
function item(diary, g, it, x, y, w) {
  const mid = y + LINE / 2;
  if (it.kind === 'bar') {
    barStyle(g, it.key, x, y + 2, 10, LINE - 4);
    text(g, it.text, x + 14, mid, w - 14, 'italic 11px Homerton',
      FAINT);
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O15Diary" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
The ref is <CODE>{ section: 'planner', id }</CODE>, so the rest follows from chapters 12 and 13: double-click a
bar in the Diary to go to it in the Planner (its <CODE>go</CODE> turns to the bar's year), choose it and press
Delete, drag it into the wastebasket, or link it to something.<P>
The other way, double-clicking a day on the chart goes to that day in the Diary:<P>
<PRE>
  double(ev) {
    const day = this.dayAt(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (day) this.org.go({ section: 'diary', date: day });
    return true;
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>org.go</CODE> gives the Diary a ref with a date and no id; the Diary's <CODE>go</CODE> (chapter 12)
turns to the week of <CODE>ref.date</CODE>.<P>
Finally the Planner takes its place in the binder, and with it every tab has a section of its own - the last
<CODE>blank</CODE> is gone:<P>
<PRE>
    this.sections = [DiaryPage, ToDoPage, AddressPage, NotesPage,
      PlannerPage, AnniversaryPage].map((S) =&gt; new S(this));
</PRE>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Turning the year</H2>
The Planner shows one year. Its page corners turn to the year before or after, Today (the toolbox, or Home)
goes back to this year. Turning isn't remembered for Back, but leaving the section is, and
<CODE>state</CODE> gives the year to remember, so Back from the Diary returns to the year you were looking at:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ entries
  turn(dir) { this.year += dir; }
  today() { this.year = +D.today().slice(0, 4); }
  describe(ref) {
    const b = this.doc.planner.get(ref.id);
    return b ? `${PLANNER_KEY[b.key]}, ${showDay(b.from)} to ` +
      showDay(b.to) : 'Planner bar';
  }
  remove(ref) { this.doc.planner.remove(ref.id); }
  go(ref) {
    const b = this.doc.planner.get(ref.id);
    if (b) this.year = +b.from.slice(0, 4);
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>describe</CODE> - used by the "Delete ...?" question and the links menu - says what a bar is:
<I>Project A, 05/10/2026 to 23/10/2026</I>.<P>

