to see next year's (empty) planner, and the left-hand one to come back.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>A planner bar is a kind and two day keys; <CODE>onDay</CODE> is two string comparisons.<P>
<LI>Work out a chart's sizes in one place (<CODE>grid</CODE>) and use them both to draw it and to find what's
under the pointer.<P>
<LI>Give colours patterns as well, so they can be told apart without colour.<P>
<LI><CODE>save</CODE>, <CODE>clip</CODE> and <CODE>restore</CODE> confine drawing to a shape;
<CODE>rgba(r, g, b, a)</CODE> is a colour you can see through.<P>
<LI>A drag that doesn't carry an entry goes to the section's own <CODE>drag</CODE>;
<CODE>wimp.drag({ type: 'point' })</CODE> waits for where the pointer is let go.<P>
<LI>Always give the mouse's jobs a keyboard way too: here, New and a form.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Show how many days each bar covers in the Diary's week view, after its name (<I>Project A (15
days)</I>). <CODE>D.daysBetween</CODE> counts the days between two keys.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Let the user rename the six kinds. Keep the names in the document (so each organiser can have its
own) instead of <CODE>PLANNER_KEY</CODE>, and change one by double-clicking its sample in the key.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Let a bar be moved: dragging a bar to another day on the chart should move it there, keeping its
length.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Lotus Organizer's planner folded open. Add a view button, <I>Half</I>, that shows only the six
months around today, three on each page, with wider columns.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  In <CODE>DiaryPage.itemsOn</CODE>, give the bar its length in its text:
<PRE>
text: `${PLANNER_KEY[b.key]} (${D.daysBetween(b.from, b.to) + 1}` +
  ' days)',
</PRE>
(+ 1 because a bar from Monday to Monday covers the first day too).<P>
<DT><DD>2  Keep <CODE>doc.plannerNames</CODE> (saved with the rest of the file, defaulting to a copy of
<CODE>PLANNER_KEY</CODE>), use it wherever <CODE>PLANNER_KEY[k]</CODE> is used, and make
<CODE>PlannerPage.double</CODE> <CODE>async</CODE> (it now waits for a form), adding before it looks for a
day:
<PRE>
const k = this.keys.find((r) =&gt; inside(r, ev.x, ev.y));
if (k) {
  const v = await this.nameForm().ask({
    name: this.doc.plannerNames[k.k] });
  if (v) this.doc.renameKind(k.k, v.name);
  return true;
}
</PRE>
where <CODE>nameForm</CODE> makes (once, with <CODE>??=</CODE>) a one-field Form like New's, and
<CODE>renameKind</CODE> changes the name and marks the document changed.<P>
<DT><DD>3  A bar dragged onto the pages already goes to the section's <CODE>dropped</CODE> (chapter 12),
which the Planner hasn't got. Give it one:
<PRE>
dropped(ref, x, y) {
  const day = this.dayAt(x, y), b = this.doc.planner.get(ref.id);
  if (!day || !b) return;
  const len = D.daysBetween(b.from, b.to);
  this.doc.planner.update(b.id, { from: day,
    to: D.addDays(day, len) });
}
</PRE>
The bar's first day goes where it's dropped, wherever on the bar the drag began; a better version would
remember which day was under the pointer when the drag started.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Give the section <CODE>views: ['Year', 'Half']</CODE> in its constructor, and make <CODE>grid</CODE> and
<CODE>draw</CODE> use the months to show instead of 1 and 7: for <I>Half</I>, the month three before this one
and the one after it, three rows each, so <CODE>rowH</CODE> is worked out for three rows. <CODE>dayAt</CODE>
uses the same two first months, so clicks still find the right days.<P>
